 The ProofRail port takes a versioned context envelope containing the task contract, parent snapshot, confirmed decisions, latest failure evidence, unresolved tickets, budgets, authorized targets, allowed effects and reference hashes. It returns external execution facts and candidate references, never authoritative task PASS. An `agent-runner-request` identifies a call by `requestId/runId/taskId/stepId/attempt`, binds immutable input through `workspaceHash/contextHash/parentSnapshotHash/authorizationHash/budgetHash`, and explicitly lists `allowedTargets/allowedEffects`. `authorizationHash` must bind exactly one authorization-grant `recordHash`; admission resolves that unique grant by hash, then evaluates the authorization ledger by `authorizationId` for active/unrevoked/unexpired state, and requires `runId/taskId/stepId/allowedTargets` to match the request. `budgetHash` must bind this request's cost `reservationHash`, not a `ledgerHash` or allocation hash; the reservation must remain outstanding (no settlement), and its `runId/requestId/authorizationHash` must match the request. `allowedEffects` are operation effect IDs while grant `effectClasses` are risk classes. Each requested operation must exist in the request-bound frozen mapping version, and every mapped class must occur in the grant's `effectClasses`; grant supersets are allowed. Unknown, missing, or tampered mapping versions or hashes, unknown operations, and class under-coverage fail closed before replay identity consumption or dispatch. These binding semantics are frozen jointly by the Schema, canonical vector, semantic fixtures, and Go admission, but this does not mean the T027 production AgentRunner is implemented or that AT-23 passes. Production admission is a pure preflight and immutable binding: it must not consume request replay identities, publish R/C or write to the replay store, or launch processes; first-dispatch eligibility and replay identity consumption belong to the dispatch path and are decided after R has been published, based on the replay store; the admission verdict is a point-in-time preflight conclusion, not a lock, and may expire before dispatch, so after publishing R and before allowing launch the dispatch path must reconfirm that the authorization has not been revoked and that the budget reservation is still outstanding, failing closed otherwise, and a prior admission pass must never be treated as a launch permit. `mode=create` cannot carry a prior session; its first event establishes the request's sole session binding. `mode=resume` must bind `priorSessionId` and `priorCompletionHash`. Persist the request and authorization digest before launch. The same requestId and digest may replay, while a different digest conflicts; duplicate requests must not restart an agent in an unknown state or reapply the same candidate. A new business attempt receives new identity and cost accounting. Resume binds the original attempt, session and workspace; when continuity cannot be proven, reconstruct from the durable envelope as a new attempt rather than guessing that resume succeeded. Missing links, duplicates, digest mismatches, settled reservations, expired/revoked grants, or scope mismatches fail closed.
 
-Before any AI-assisted entry point starts, ProofRail must perform a fail-closed availability preflight for the provider profile and channel that will actually be used. A profile stores only provider type, base URL, model, transport options, and an opaque `secretRef`; API keys and tokens cannot enter configuration, digests, logs, errors, or evidence, and may be resolved only from the system SecretStore for temporary injection into the child-process environment. `agent-runner-cli`, `sessionbridge-silent`, and `sessionbridge-visible` each produce an independent `available`, `unavailable`, or `unknown` verdict that cannot be inherited by another channel. Valid configuration, an existing secret, an installed CLI/extension, reachable IPC, or successful visible delivery is insufficient to prove AI availability. `available` requires a fresh, evidence-backed minimal live response on that channel, bound to the profile configuration digest, channel, and probe time, and executed within an explicit request budget (the CLI-side credit bound is a candidate-platform fact that may exceed the product request budget; the actual argument is proven by the argv digest of the probe evidence, while the product request budget is still enforced independently and the request count is recorded). **This record carries no cost dimension, and that absence must not be read as "no cost was incurred"** — actual CLI-side billing and call counts are recorded by the candidate platform; the product side does not double-count them and makes no guarantee about the monetary bill; stale, timed-out, skipped, or incomplete evidence blocks. Failure reasons include at least `configuration_invalid`, `secret_missing`, `credential_rejected`, `account_unavailable`, `quota_exhausted`, `network_unreachable`, `tls_or_proxy_failure`, `provider_unavailable`, `model_unavailable`, `policy_blocked`, `cli_unavailable`, `sessionbridge_unavailable`, `lm_api_unavailable`, `visible_delivery_unavailable`, `response_invalid`, `timeout`, and `unknown`. An unavailable channel cannot automatically switch provider, model, or channel. Non-AI features may continue, while the affected AI entry point remains disabled and displays its structured reason.
+Before any AI-assisted entry point starts, ProofRail must perform a fail-closed availability preflight for the provider profile and channel that will actually be used. A profile stores only provider type, base URL, model, transport options, and an opaque `secretRef`; API keys and tokens cannot enter configuration, digests, logs, errors, or evidence, and may be resolved only from the system SecretStore for temporary injection into the child-process environment. `agent-runner-cli`, `sessionbridge-silent`, and `sessionbridge-visible` each produce an independent `available`, `unavailable`, or `unknown` verdict that cannot be inherited by another channel. Valid configuration, an existing secret, an installed CLI/extension, reachable IPC, or successful visible delivery is insufficient to prove AI availability. `available` requires a fresh, evidence-backed minimal live response on that channel, bound to the profile configuration digest, channel, and probe time, and executed within an explicit request budget (the CLI-side credit bound is a candidate-platform fact that may exceed the product request budget; the actual argument is proven by the argv digest of the probe evidence, while the product request budget is still enforced independently and the request count is recorded). **This record carries no cost dimension, and that absence must not be read as "no cost was incurred"** — actual CLI-side billing and call counts are recorded by the candidate platform; the product side does not double-count them and makes no guarantee about the monetary bill; stale, timed-out, skipped, or incomplete evidence blocks. Failure reasons include at least `configuration_invalid`, `secret_missing`, `credential_rejected`, `account_unavailable`, `quota_exhausted`, `network_unreachable`, `tls_or_proxy_failure`, `provider_unavailable`, `model_unavailable`, `policy_blocked`, `cli_unavailable`, `sessionbridge_unavailable`, `lm_api_unavailable`, `visible_delivery_unavailable`, `response_invalid`, `timeout`, `byok_confinement_unproven`, and `unknown`. An unavailable channel cannot automatically switch provider, model, or channel. Non-AI features may continue, while the affected AI entry point remains disabled and displays its structured reason.
+
+For availability probing of the `agent-runner-cli` channel, the install source of the probe executable must be provably immutable: on Windows it is named by a stable absolute path and the ACL of its install directory denies write and delete to the current user's token; on Linux every component of its install directory path is not writable by the current user, and the executable is either ELF or its shebang interpreter satisfies the same immutability condition; all other platforms fail closed. The proof is persisted as the store-local `ai-install-source-proof.json` artifact under a path derived from the chain configuration directory (written by the ProofRail check command, read by the check command and the probe executor, re-read and re-verified before every start for path, file identity, and digest; callers cannot choose its path). If the install source cannot be proven, `available` must not be recorded and the verdict is refused as `cli_unavailable`. Windows ACL and Linux mode/owner evaluation are owned by platform-specific implementations, and any failed evaluation fails closed.
 
 A `windows-credential:<target>` reference uses a generic credential in the current user's Windows Credential Manager with local-machine persistence; it is neither runtime isolation nor proof of provider availability. `prfrail secret set` may read a secret only from a verified local console with echo disabled; argv, redirection, pipes, JSON request bodies, and ordinary stdin injection are forbidden. `status` returns only the reference and existence, `delete` is idempotent, and none of the three commands displays the secret. An adapter receives only a temporary resolved byte copy and clears it after use. System failures return stable classifications without credential-blob content. Non-Windows platforms fail closed until their own SecretStore backend exists; they do not share or infer Windows credentials.
 
+In secret authentication mode, probing `agent-runner-cli` must not resolve credentials and must not start the child process when it cannot be proven that the child cannot write unredacted output to disk (zero-start BYOK refusal): the proof basis is a whitelisted argv with no log destination, a whitelisted environment, inheritance of standard stream handles only, a confined working directory with bounded in-memory output, plus platform write-restriction evaluation in the sandbox check layer; if any item cannot be proven the verdict is refused, and refusal evidence uses only stable closed-set classifications. The host-auth path is not subject to this gate.
+
 The optional top-level `ai` section of a chain configuration persists only `profiles[]` and `channels[]`. Each profile uses the non-secret `profileId/providerType/baseUrl/model/wireApi/authMode/secretRef` fields and has an independent configuration digest under `proofrail:ai-provider-profile:1\n`. Each `channels[]` entry contains only `channel/profileId`, where channel is one of `agent-runner-cli|sessionbridge-silent|sessionbridge-visible`. Both arrays are lexically ordered by primary key and reject duplicates. Every channel references a declared profile; an unbound channel remains unconfigured and fails closed rather than inheriting another channel. Host authentication cannot carry `secretRef`; secret authentication requires an opaque reference and HTTPS base URL. `config explain` may expose profileId, the profile configuration digest, and channel bindings, but never `secretRef`, credential existence, or any resolved value. Static `validate/config explain/preview` performs no SecretStore read and no availability probe.
 
 The formal AI execution channel is a CLI Agent adapter implementing the `AgentRunner` port. An adapter maps the unified request to an external CLI pinned by version and digest; it never promotes vendor transcripts, private session formats or exit codes directly into core state. Before launch it must probe and record normalized `os`/`arch`, the CLI platform-package/build fingerprint `platformHash`, executable identity/version, noninteractive invocation, working-directory binding, machine-readable events or complete logs, session create/resume, cancellation and process-tree termination, tool/network/permission controls, cost usage, and unattended confirmation behavior. Candidate identity includes at least version, `os`, `arch`, `platformHash`, `executableHash`, and `configHash`; another platform running the same version cannot inherit its conclusions. `platformHash` alone does not prove a kernel, filesystem, or OS enforcement boundary; enforcement configuration and runtime evidence retain that responsibility. A missing required capability blocks preflight. Prompt promises cannot substitute for OS or runner enforcement.
